.TH HL 1
.SH NAME
hl, ts \- tree-sitter's colors in acme's windows
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hl
[
.B -a
.I acme
] [
.B -s
.I astyles
] [
.B -t
.I tsdir
]
.PP
.BI /bin/ts/ lang
[
.B -q
.I highlights.scm
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Hl
colors the text of
.IR acme (1)'s
windows by its syntax, as tree-sitter's grammars parse it,
in each window's
.IR astyles (4)
layer
.BR treesitter .
It waits for
.BR $acmesrv ,
an acme's
.B /srv
file, and follows its windows, mounted on
.I acme
.RB ( /mnt/acme ),
writing their layers in
.I astyles
.RB ( /mnt/astyles ).
.PP
A window whose file the first rule of
.B $home/lib/ts
or
.B /lib/ts
matches is colored by that rule's grammar.
A rule is a line,
.BI ts= name
.BI file= pattern
[
.BI fence= pattern
]\f1,
where
.B file=
is a
.IR regexp (6)
of the file's name, and
.BR fence= ,
of the other names of the rule's language,
as a fenced block in markdown names it.
.I Hl
gives a window's grammar its body once, under acme's
.B seq
guard, and then its edits, from the window's
.BR rdevent ,
and writes in its layer only the runs that the grammar says may have changed,
each under the
.B seq
of the edits it reflects, so that
.I astyles
refuses colors for text that has since changed,
and
.I hl
colors that text again after the next edits.
A Get, an edit whose text
.B rdevent
leaves out, or a grammar that has lost the document
has
.I hl
give the body again.
.PP
A grammar's program gives the text of a language it does not have,
such as a markdown file's fenced block of code, as one run,
.BI ts/ lang\f1,
or
.BI ts/ lang / name
for the run that the text takes when there is no program for
.IR lang .
.I Hl
gives that text to
.IR lang 's
program, the grammar of the first rule whose
.I name
is
.I lang
or whose
.B fence=
pattern matches it, or else
.BI /bin/ts/ lang
if there is one,
and puts the program's runs where the text is.
The program gets the block's edits as the window's grammar does,
and a block whose bounds an edit crosses is given again.
So a block marked
.B sh
is colored as
.BR bash .
.PP
Each grammar is a program,
.IB tsdir / name
.RB ( /bin/ts/ name ),
that
.I hl
starts once and keeps for every window in its language.
It reads requests on its standard input and answers some on its standard output:
.TF delete\ id\ q0\ q1
.TP
.BI body " id n"
followed by
.I n
bytes, the text of document
.IR id ;
answered by
.BI runs " id m"
and
.I m
bytes of runs, in the form of
.IR astyles (4)'s
.B style
file.
The program keeps each document's parse, so that a change is parsed again only where it is.
.TP
.BI insert " id q n"
followed by
.I n
bytes, put in document
.I id
at rune
.IR q .
.TP
.BI delete " id q0 q1"
takes the runes
.I q0
to
.I q1
from it.
.TP
.BI span " id q0 q1"
answered by
.BI span " id r0 r1 m"
and
.I m
bytes of the runs of the runes
.I r0
to
.IR r1 ,
counted from
.IR r0 :
the text whose runs may have changed since the last answer, and that of
.I q0
to
.IR q1 ;
or by
.BI stale " id"
if an edit could not be made.
.TP
.BI tree " id n"
followed by
.I n
bytes, the text of document
.IR id ;
answered by
.BI tree " id m"
and
.I m
bytes: a line for each named node of the parse, root first and each node before its children,
.IP
.IB "depth start length type field"
.IP
where
.I depth
counts the named nodes above it,
.I start
and
.I length
are in runes,
and
.I field
is the name the grammar gives the node's place in its parent, or
.LR - .
.TP
.BI "edit " "id q0 q1 n"
followed by
.I n
bytes, which take the place of runes
.I q0
to
.I q1
of document
.IR id 's
text as the program keeps it.
The program does not answer it.
.TP
.BI tree " id"
with no text, answered by
.BI delta " id m"
and
.I m
bytes: what changed in the parse since the last answer to
.BR tree ,
as hunks (see below).
.TP
.BI check " id n"
followed by
.I n
bytes, a text parsed alone and not kept; answered by
.BI check " id k\f1,"
where
.I k
counts the parse's error and missing nodes.
.TP
.BI tags " id n"
followed by
.I n
bytes, the text of document
.IR id ,
or with no
.I n
the text as the program keeps it;
answered by
.BI tags " id m"
and
.I m
bytes: a line for each match of the language's
.BR tags.scm ,
.IP
.IB "start length type kind name-start name-length"
.IP
where
.I kind
is the match's capture, such as
.B definition.function
or
.BR reference.call ,
and the name is its
.B @name
capture's text;
or, if the language has no
.BR tags.scm ,
by
.BI error " id why"\f1.
.TP
.BI close " id"
forgets document
.IR id .
.PD
.PP
The program keeps a document's parse from one request to the next, whichever they are.
For the C text
.LR "int x = f(1);" ,
.B tree
gives
.IP
.EX
0 0 14 translation_unit -
1 0 13 declaration -
2 0 3 primitive_type type
2 4 8 init_declarator declarator
3 4 1 identifier declarator
3 8 4 call_expression value
4 8 1 identifier function
4 9 3 argument_list arguments
5 10 1 number_literal -
.EE
.PP
which tree-sitter writes as
.IP
.EX
(translation_unit (declaration type: (primitive_type)
  declarator: (init_declarator declarator: (identifier)
    value: (call_expression function: (identifier)
      arguments: (argument_list (number_literal))))))
.EE
.PP
A
.B delta
answer is the last
.B tree
or
.B delta
answer brought up to date.
Each line of that answer not in a hunk stays, with its start and end moved by the
.BR edit s
since, in order: an
.B edit
of
.I q0
to
.I q1
with
.I k
runes of new text moves a place before
.I q0
nowhere, a place after
.I q1
by
.IR k \-( q1 \- q0 ),
a start at
.IR q1 ,
or an end there if
.I q0
is less than
.IR q1 ,
the same way, and any other place to
.IR q0 .
So an insert at
.I q0
moves a node that starts there and does not lengthen one that ends there.
A hunk is a line
.IB "@ i k j"
followed by
.I j
lines in
.BR tree 's
form, which take the place of the
.I k
lines from line
.I i
(from 0) of the last answer; hunks come in the order of
.IR i .
If the program does not have the text, as for an
.B edit
past its end, it answers
.B tree
and
.B tags
without text with
.BI error " id " lost\f1,
and wants the text again, as by
.BI tree " id n"\f1.
.PP
Any program that speaks this, such as one for a small format written by hand, can stand in for one.
.B /bin/ts/pic
is one, for
.IR pic (1)'s
pictures: their words, labels, numbers, strings and comments.
It has no parse, and answers
.B tree
and
.B tags
with an error;
.B edit
and
.B check
are only for grammars' programs.
.PP
A grammar's program colors the captures of its query,
.IB lang /highlights.scm\f1,
by tree-sitter's query language with its
.BR #eq? ,
.B #match?
and
.B #any-of?
predicates and their
.B not-
forms.
Where captures overlap, the first one wins.
A capture's run takes the name that
.B names
gives its capture, or the capture with its last
.BI . part
taken off, and so on; a capture with no name has no run.
Each of these files is read from
.B $home/lib/ts
in place of
.BR /sys/lib/ts .
The
.B -q
option reads the query from a file instead.
.SH FILES
.TF /sys/lib/ts/lang/highlights.scm
.TP
.B /lib/ts
the languages' rules
.TP
.B /sys/lib/ts/names
capture names to
.IR theme (6)
names
.TP
.BI /sys/lib/ts/ lang /highlights.scm
a language's query
.TP
.BI /sys/lib/ts/ lang /tags.scm
its definitions and references, for
.BR tags ,
from the grammar's own repository
.TP
.BI /bin/ts/ lang
a language's program
.PD
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.BR src/cmd/hl.c ,
.B src/cmd/ts/ts.c
and
.BR src/cmd/ts/pic.c ;
tree-sitter's runtime and grammars from
.BR scripts/dl.sh ,
and the queries and names from acme-treesitter
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR acme (1),
.IR Tree (1),
.IR astyles (4),
.IR theme (6)
